\section{Removing Binders by Bracket Abstraction}

Combinator translation replaces a lexical binding mechanism with a small set of application rules. With application left-associative, use \(I\,u\to u\), \(K\,u\,v\to u\), and \(S\,f\,g\,u\to(f\,u)(g\,u)\). To translate a lambda, first translate its body and then abstract the parameter \(x\) from the result. For a term \(t\) that may still contain other free variables, define
\[
\begin{aligned}
 [x]x &= I,\\
 [x]t &= K\,t &&\text{if \(x\) is not free in \(t\)},\\
 [x](p\,q) &= S\,([x]p)\,([x]q) &&\text{otherwise}.
\end{aligned}
\]
No runtime instruction searches for a name in this representation. Induction on the abstraction cases shows the key invariant: \(([x]t)\,u\) reduces to \(t[u/x]\). The application case follows by distributing \(u\) to both abstracted subterms and invoking the induction hypotheses. The argument applies to capture-free substitution; here the term being traversed has no remaining lambda binders. Nested lambdas are handled from the inside out.

This basis is compact as a specification, not necessarily as a compiled representation. For example, application with just one parameter-dependent side need not use two separate abstractions and a dummy argument discard. The combinators \(B\,f\,g\,x\to f(g\,x)\) and \(C\,f\,g\,x\to(f\,x)\,g\) handle the cases where only the right or left side depends on \(x\), respectively. More generally, a compiler can generate a wider combinator with a named code entry and arity for a whole body. The choice trades generated code size against the number of dispatched reductions.

\section{Worked Example: Duplicate Edges, Not Computations}

Let \(\mathit{add}\) be a strict curried primitive and \(E\) a closed computation. Abstraction turns \((\lambda x.\,\mathit{add}\,x\,x)\,E\) into
\[
 (S\,(S\,(K\,\mathit{add})\,I)\,I)\,E.
\]
The outer \(S\) contracts to \(((S\,(K\,\mathit{add})\,I)\,E)(I\,E)\). The inner \(S\), \(K\), and \(I\) contractions expose \(\mathit{add}\,E\,E\). If a reducer builds a tree by copying \(E\), each operand can repeat its work. A graph reducer instead inserts two edges to the original node for \(E\). Both operand paths must eventually encounter that node, even when intermediate \(I\) redexes are replaced. For \(E=\mathit{add}\,20\,1\), forcing the first operand produces \(21\) and updates the shared computation; forcing the other reuses \(21\). The answer is \(42\) with two primitive additions, rather than three.

The two edges are justified by the redex's single dynamic argument, not by a search for textually equal expressions. Chapter~\ref{ch:ext-174} explains how a heap update preserves this identity. Conversely, duplicating the edges does not entitle the reducer to evaluate the argument immediately. A weak normal-order implementation must allow \(K\,7\,\Omega\) to return \(7\) even if \(\Omega\) diverges. Test duplication and discard separately: the former needs a reduction counter or heap trace to detect lost sharing, while the latter can be checked by observing that execution returns without entering the discarded argument.

Do not confuse this runtime identity with the congruence classes in the equality-saturation proposal of \emph{The Modern Compiler Dossier}, Chapter~10. An e-class records interchangeable expressions under a chosen equivalence; it does not allocate a thunk or define its lifetime. Extracting identical expressions at two program points need not create one shared evaluation. To obtain that effect, the compiler still needs a binding or another way to route both demands to one dynamic node.

\section{Sharing Across Calls}

Paul Hudak and David Kranz's \emph{A Combinator-based Compiler for a Functional Language} discusses both locating potentially shared computations in a macro-combinator graph and using strictness information to avoid unnecessary lazy treatment. Their example of a factorial depending on a captured variable illustrates a different scope of sharing. In a closure for \(\lambda n.\,\mathit{test}\,n\,(\mathit{fac}\,x)\), the factorial depends on the environment's \(x\), not on \(n\). A full-laziness transformation can place one suspended factorial computation in each such environment and reuse it across calls of that closure. It cannot place a single global result in the code block if distinct closure instances capture different values of \(x\).

That optimization is a lifetime decision as well as an equation. Hoisting an expression out of a lambda can retain everything it captures, even when the result is rarely demanded. Hoisting it as an \emph{eager} computation can also introduce divergence at closure construction or on a branch that never uses it. Compare two retained closures with different captured values and alternate calls that do and do not demand the factorial. A useful performance record distinguishes the number of allocated suspension nodes, the number entered, and the peak live graph. A reduction-count improvement alone does not establish that wider sharing saves memory.
